= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <equilibria> have $y=0$ and $x(\lambda+x^2)=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{O=(0,0)\text{ for all parameters},\qquad P_\pm=(\pm\sqrt{-\lambda},0)\text{ for }\lambda<0.}
$$
At $O$, the <linearization> has <trace> $\kappa$ and <determinant> $\lambda$. Hence $O$ is a <saddle equilibrium> when $\lambda<0$, a <sink equilibrium> when $\lambda>0,\kappa<0$, and a <source equilibrium> when $\lambda>0,\kappa>0$. The <sink equilibrium>/<source equilibrium> may be a <node equilibrium> or a <focus equilibrium>; the node-focus discriminant is not a <bifurcation> curve. At $P_\pm$, the <trace> and <determinant> are $\kappa+\lambda$ and $-2\lambda$. These points are <sink equilibria> for $\kappa<-\lambda$ and <source equilibria> for $\kappa>-\lambda$.

The local <bifurcation> curves are therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda=0\quad\text{(pitchfork)},\qquad
\kappa=0,\ \lambda>0\quad\text{(Hopf at }O),\qquad
\kappa=-\lambda,\ \lambda<0\quad\text{(Hopf at }P_\pm).}
$$
For $\kappa\ne0$ on the pitchfork curve, a leading centre-manifold equation is $\dot x=(\lambda x+x^3)/\kappa+\cdots$. For $\kappa<0$, with unfolding parameter $-\lambda$, the pitchfork is supercritical and creates two <sink equilibria> from a <sink equilibrium> that becomes a <saddle equilibrium>. For $\kappa>0$ it is subcritical in the centre direction; the nonzero branches are unstable, and the transverse <eigenvalue> is also positive. The origin of parameter space is a reflection-symmetric double-zero degeneration.

To determine Hopf criticality at the origin, use the <energy> $E=y^2/2+\lambda x^2/2+x^4/4$, whose <derivative> is $(\kappa-x^2)y^2$. For a small nearly harmonic oscillation $x=R\cos(\sqrt\lambda\,t)$, averaging gives $\dot R=\kappa R/2-R^3/8+\cdots$. Thus this <Hopf bifurcation> is supercritical and creates an attracting <periodic orbit> for $\kappa>0$. By the supplied opposite-criticality fact, the simultaneous <Hopf bifurcations> at the two nonzero <equilibria> are subcritical. A direct calculation confirms this: writing $x=\sqrt{-\lambda}+X$, $Y=-y/\sqrt{-2\lambda}$ puts the cubic radial coefficient at $1/4>0$, including the contribution from the quadratic terms.

The figure includes these local curves and the global curves derived below. All lines emerging from the double-zero point are local asymptotic sketches.
\Image[paper-56-symmetric-parameters.png]{title=Local and global curves near the symmetric double-zero point}

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

With primes denoting differentiation in $\tau$, the weighted scaling gives
$$
\boxed{u'=v,\qquad v'=-\alpha u-u^3+\varepsilon(\beta-u^2)v.}
$$
Indeed $\dot x=\varepsilon^2u'$ and $\dot y=\varepsilon^3v'$, so the damping terms carry one extra power of $\varepsilon$. Consequently
$$
H=\frac12v^2+\frac{\alpha}{2}u^2+\frac14u^4,\qquad
H'=\varepsilon(\beta-u^2)v^2.
$$
At $\varepsilon=0$ this proves conservation of $H$ directly.

For $\alpha=-a_0^2<0$, the potential is a double well. Its minima are at $u=\pm a_0$, where $H=-a_0^4/4$; these are centre <equilibria>. The origin is a <saddle equilibrium> and has $H=0$. Energies $-a_0^4/4<H<0$ give two separate closed contours, one in each well. Energies $H>0$ give a single closed contour surrounding both wells. The intervening figure-eight consists of two homoclinic loops:
$$
\boxed{H_{\rm hom}=0,\qquad v^2=a_0^2u^2-\frac12u^4.}
$$
Their outer turning points are $u=\pm\sqrt2a_0$. The positive loop, for example, is parametrized by $u=\sqrt2a_0\operatorname{sech}(a_0\tau)$, $v=u'$.
\Image[paper-56-double-well-contours.png]{title=Conserved-energy contours and the two homoclinic loops}

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A returning orbit must have zero net change of the <energy>. To first order, integrate the perturbation along either unperturbed homoclinic loop:
$$
0=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(\beta-u_h^2)v_h^2\,d\tau
=\beta I_0-I_2.
$$
For the positive loop, $v_h=\pm u_h\sqrt{a_0^2-u_h^2/2}$; the outward and inward halves contribute equally. Therefore
$$
I_0=2\int_0^{\sqrt2a_0}u\sqrt{a_0^2-u^2/2}\,du=\frac43a_0^3,
$$
and
$$
I_2=2\int_0^{\sqrt2a_0}u^3\sqrt{a_0^2-u^2/2}\,du=\frac{16}{15}a_0^5.
$$
The <integrals> follow by substituting $w=a_0^2-u^2/2$; for the second one use $u^2=2(a_0^2-w)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\beta=\frac45a_0^2=-\frac45\alpha,\qquad
\kappa_{\rm hom}=-\frac45\lambda+o(|\lambda|),\quad\lambda<0.}
$$
The equality in rescaled parameters is the leading Melnikov balance, not an assertion that the entire finite-parameter <bifurcation> curve is exactly straight. Reflection <symmetry> makes both <saddle equilibrium> loops occur together. The global curve lies below the nonzero-equilibrium Hopf line $\kappa=-\lambda$, as shown in the parameter sketch.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The relevant <saddle equilibrium> is the origin, with <eigenvalues>
$$
m_\pm=\frac{\kappa\pm\sqrt{\kappa^2-4\lambda}}2.
$$
For $\lambda<0$, they have opposite signs. Its <saddle index> is
$$
\boxed{\delta=-\frac{m_-}{m_+}
=\frac{\sqrt{\kappa^2-4\lambda}-\kappa}{\sqrt{\kappa^2-4\lambda}+\kappa}<1}
$$
near the homoclinic curve, because that curve has $\kappa>0$. Along its leading asymptote, $\delta=1-\tfrac45\sqrt{-\lambda}+o(\sqrt{-\lambda})$.

A local passage near the <saddle equilibrium> contributes a power $s^\delta$ to the <return map>. Because $\delta<1$, its <derivative> becomes large as the orbit approaches the <separatrix>. The <periodic orbits> meeting the loops are therefore unstable, and their periods diverge logarithmically. On the side between the homoclinic and subcritical Hopf curves there are two unstable single-well <periodic orbits>, surrounding the two <sink equilibria>. On crossing the double-loop curve downward, they join into one unstable outer <periodic orbit> surrounding both wells. The attracting outer <periodic orbit> is not the one that collides with the <saddle equilibrium>: it persists across this global event.

The unstable outer <periodic orbit> must subsequently meet that attracting outer <periodic orbit> in a fold of <periodic orbits>. This extra curve is required to obtain the six-region diagram. One can see its position from the same energy-balance calculation. After normalizing $a_0=1$, an outer Hamiltonian contour with $H>0$ selects
$$
\beta=B(H)=\frac{\int_0^{u_{\max}}u^2\sqrt{2H+u^2-u^4/2}\,du}
{\int_0^{u_{\max}}\sqrt{2H+u^2-u^4/2}\,du},
\qquad u_{\max}^2=1+\sqrt{1+4H}.
$$
At $H=0$, $B=4/5$. The <derivative> of the denominator diverges positively there, while that of the numerator stays finite, so $B$ initially decreases. Rescaling at large $H$ gives $B(H)\propto\sqrt H$, so it eventually increases. At its minimum the stable and unstable selected <periodic orbits> meet. Thus the <outer-cycle fold in a weakly perturbed double-well oscillator> has $\kappa_{\rm fold}=c_f(-\lambda)+o(|\lambda|)$ with $0<c_f<4/5$; quadrature gives $c_f\simeq0.75226$. The decreasing portion of $B(H)$ selects the unstable <periodic orbit>, and the increasing portion selects the stable one, since the <energy> drift has sign $\beta-B(H)$.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Write $F=\kappa_{\rm fold}(\lambda)$, $G=\kappa_{\rm hom}(\lambda)$ and $H=-\lambda$ for $\lambda<0$, so $0<F<G<H$ near the double-zero point. The six open regions are:

* I: $\lambda>0,\kappa<0$. The origin is the only <equilibrium> and is attracting; there is no <periodic orbit>.
* II: $\lambda>0,\kappa>0$. The origin is repelling and is surrounded by an attracting <periodic orbit>, born at its <supercritical Hopf bifurcation>.
* III: $\lambda<0,\kappa<F$. The origin is a <saddle equilibrium> and the two nonzero <equilibria> are <sink equilibria>. Its <stable manifold> separates their basins; there is no <periodic orbit>.
* IV: $\lambda<0,F<\kappa<G$. There are two <sink equilibria> and the <saddle equilibrium>, an unstable <periodic orbit> surrounding all three, and a larger attracting <periodic orbit>. Initial conditions inside the unstable <periodic orbit> go to one of the <sink equilibria>, according to the <saddle equilibrium>'s basin boundary; those outside it approach the attracting <periodic orbit>.
* V: $\lambda<0,G<\kappa<H$. The <saddle equilibrium> and two <sink equilibria> remain, but each <sink equilibrium> is surrounded by its own unstable <periodic orbit>. A larger attracting <periodic orbit> surrounds the entire configuration. Each unstable inner <periodic orbit> bounds its <sink equilibrium>'s basin.
* VI: $\lambda<0,\kappa>H$. The two nonzero <equilibria> are repelling. The <saddle equilibrium>'s <stable manifold> still leads to the <saddle equilibrium>, while generic trajectories approach the attracting outer <periodic orbit>.

The changes III–IV, IV–V and V–VI are respectively a fold of <periodic orbits>, the double homoclinic event, and the two <subcritical Hopf bifurcations>. Thus the unstable <periodic orbits> from the <subcritical Hopf bifurcations>, the expanding <saddle equilibrium> passage, and the attracting <periodic orbit> from the other <Hopf bifurcation> fit into one consistent portrait.
\Image[paper-56-six-portraits.png]{title=The six local phase-portrait types; solid cycles attract and dashed cycles repel}

= 2
